  "mechanisms": [
    "The Yerkes-Dodson Law: The relationship between arousal (stimulus strength) and performance (rapidity of habit-formation) is curvilinear, with optimal performance occurring at an intermediate level of arousal. This optimal level shifts depending on task difficulty.",
    "Motivation: Weak stimuli provide insufficient motivation for learning, while excessively strong stimuli may lead to distress or interfere with learning.",
    "Cognitive load: More difficult discrimination tasks require higher cognitive effort, making the organism more sensitive to arousal levels."
  ],
  "moderators": [
    "Visual discrimination difficulty (easy, medium, difficult)"
  ],
  "implementation_implications": [
    "The study highlights the importance of matching the level of stimulation/arousal to the difficulty of a task for optimal learning or performance. For easy tasks, higher stimulation can be beneficial, but for difficult tasks, moderate or even lower stimulation is more effective."
  ],
